// Chapter XV: The Secretariat — Articles 97-101, stage E8 (18.09.2026).
// All five articles are EXECUTABLE.
//
// THE PACKAGE'S FIRST OFFICER ENTITY, `SecretariatOfficial`, AND WHY THE
// ARTICLE 8/9 ENTITY-GAP BOUNDARY DOES NOT APPLY HERE. Stage E1 declared
// Article 8 (equal eligibility of men and women) and Article 9 (five
// representatives per Member) for a stated reason: this package had never
// modelled a natural person, and introducing one for a single article with no
// other rule consuming it would be machinery bought for nothing. E1's own
// decision log closed that entry with a forward pointer — "logged so a future
// session working on Art. 97/101 (individual officers) knows where to pick
// this up." This is that session. Chapter XV is about its officers from end
// to end: Article 97 appoints the Secretary-General, Article 98 puts a
// reporting duty on him, Article 99 gives him a referral power, Article 100
// puts an independence duty on him AND on the staff, and Article 101 appoints
// the staff. FIVE articles consume the entity, four of them through rules
// written below — so the entity now earns its place on exactly the test
// Article 8/9 failed.
//
// ONE ENTITY FOR BOTH THE SECRETARY-GENERAL AND THE STAFF, WITH MEMBERSHIP OF
// THE SECRETARIAT DERIVED, NOT ASSERTED. Article 97's own first sentence
// ("The Secretariat shall comprise a Secretary-General and such staff as the
// Organization may require") is read as what it says: a definition of the
// Secretariat's population by two branches. `member_of_secretariat` is
// therefore DERIVED from `secretary_general` or from `staff_member` — two
// bases, one head, the same shape as Articles 10/11/14, Article 32's two
// subject classes and Article 93's own two bases — rather than a third case
// fact a scenario would have to assert alongside the other two (which would
// let a case report a staff member who is somehow not of the Secretariat, a
// state of affairs Article 97 excludes by definition). Article 100's
// independence duty reads that derived population, so Article 97's first
// sentence does real inferential work instead of being organizational
// scenery.
//
// ARTICLE 97's APPOINTMENT CHAIN MIRRORS ARTICLE 93, PARAGRAPH 2 AND ARTICLE
// 4 — AND ITS GENERAL ASSEMBLY STEP IS LIKEWISE A FLAT FACT. "The
// Secretary-General shall be appointed by the General Assembly upon the
// recommendation of the Security Council" is the same recommend-then-decide
// chain as Article 4, paragraph 2 (admission), Article 5 (suspension),
// Article 6 (expulsion) and Article 93, paragraph 2 (Statute conditions).
// Article 18, paragraph 2's list of "important questions" was checked and does
// NOT name the appointment of the Secretary-General, so nothing in the text
// supports a two-thirds threshold here; following Article 4's own convention
// (`general_assembly_decides_admission`, a flat empirical fact), the Assembly
// step is a flat fact rather than an unrolled vote — the threshold machinery
// is reused only where an article's own text turns on the threshold
// (Articles 5, 6, 18, 67, 89). Article 97's third sentence ("He shall be the
// chief administrative officer of the Organization") is a copula in the same
// descriptive register as Article 92's own declared "shall be the principal
// judicial organ", and is left as unformalized residue inside an otherwise
// EXECUTABLE article.
//
// ARTICLE 98 IS EXECUTABLE THROUGH ITS SECOND SENTENCE ONLY — THE SAME
// "EXECUTABLE VIA ONE NARROW DUTY" TREATMENT AS ARTICLE 73. The first
// sentence (the Secretary-General acting in that capacity in all meetings of
// four organs and performing such other functions as are entrusted to him) is
// an open-ended functional description naming no determinate act a case could
// report as done or not done — the same register as Article 74's policy
// directive. The SECOND sentence ("The Secretary-General shall make an annual
// report to the General Assembly on the work of the Organization") is a
// determinate duty with a determinate addressee and a determinate act, and
// its nearest sibling in this package is already a duty: Article 88's own
// annual report by the administering authority
// (14-trusteeship-council.law, `SubmitAnnualTrusteeshipReport`). Article 73's
// own paragraph (e) established at E7 that one narrow reporting duty is
// enough to make an otherwise-aspirational article EXECUTABLE; Article 98 is
// the same shape.
//
// ARTICLE 99 IS THIS STAGE'S NEW DECISION-0194 CHANNEL, AND IT INTRODUCES A
// NEW AUTHORITY, `SecretaryGeneral`. "The Secretary-General may bring to the
// attention of the Security Council any matter WHICH IN HIS OPINION may
// threaten the maintenance of international peace and security" delegates the
// evaluative call to a named holder in the article's own words — the plainest
// channel-1 case in the whole package, and the first one whose holder is not
// an organ but an officer. `authority SecretaryGeneral` is a new value: this
// package has previously used only `GeneralAssembly` (Art. 4, 6, 14, 19) and
// `SecurityCouncil` (Art. 31, 34, 37, 39, 94) — `authority` is a free
// identifier naming the holder of the judgment, so extending it is additive
// and needs no core change. It matters that the holder is the officer and not
// the Council: an Article 99 referral is expressly the Secretary-General's
// OWN assessment, which is precisely what distinguishes this power from
// Article 35's Member-driven referral and Article 34's Council-driven
// determination of the very same subject matter.
//
// ARTICLE 99 MODELS THE POWER, NOT ITS EXERCISE — MIRRORING ARTICLE 35 AND
// ARTICLE 96. What the article grants is an ENTITLEMENT ("may bring"), so the
// conclusion is `entitled_to_bring_matter_to_security_council_attention`,
// derived from the officer's status plus his own opinion, exactly as Article
// 35's `entitled_to_bring_dispute_to_attention` (08-pacific-settlement.law)
// and Article 96's `entitled_to_request_advisory_opinion`
// (15-international-court-of-justice.law) are. No separate "he did bring it"
// fact gates the conclusion: whether a discretionary power was exercised is
// not a condition of HAVING it, and adding such a gate would make the
// entitlement untestable in the only case where it matters (before the
// referral). The meaningful negative case is therefore the absence of the
// Secretary-General's own opinion, not the absence of a referral act.
//
// ARTICLE 99 REUSES `SecurityCouncilMatter` RATHER THAN ADDING A SIXTH
// "MATTER" ENTITY. The package already carries `Matter` (Ch. IV),
// `SecurityCouncilMatter` (Ch. V, "question or dispute under consideration by
// the Security Council"), `Situation` (Art. 39), `Dispute` (Ch. VI) and
// `EcosocMatter` (Ch. X). Article 99's object is a matter brought to the
// SECURITY COUNCIL's attention — the same kind of thing Articles 31-32
// already key their participation tests to — so it is read as that same
// entity, following Article 52's own "qualifying fact over a parallel entity"
// convention (E5) rather than the "new entity per chapter" one (E2-E4): a
// sixth near-synonym would duplicate, not distinguish.
//
// ARTICLE 100 IS A BIDIRECTIONAL DUTY, AND BOTH DIRECTIONS ARE REAL. This is
// the only article in the package whose two paragraphs place obligations on
// two DIFFERENT classes of bearer over one and the same subject matter:
// paragraph 1 on the Secretary-General and the staff (not to seek or receive
// instructions from any external government or authority, and to refrain from
// action reflecting on their position as international officials), paragraph 2
// on each Member of the United Nations (to respect the exclusively
// international character of those responsibilities and not to seek to
// influence them). Two separate duties, each with its own bearer, its own
// discharge fact and its own scenarios — folding them into one would have to
// pick one bearer and silently drop the other half of the article. Both are
// expressed in this package's standing `achieve` form over a positive
// discharge fact, the same construction every other duty here uses (Articles
// 25, 27, 33, 43, 49, 51, 52, 54, 56, 73, 84, 88, 94): a negative duty is
// discharged by the conduct the text prescribes, and a case reports that
// conduct positively — this package never expresses a duty's discharge as the
// absence of its breach.
//
// ARTICLE 101 IS EXECUTABLE THROUGH PARAGRAPH 1, WHOSE CONDITION IS GENUINE.
// "The staff shall be appointed by the Secretary-General under regulations
// established by the General Assembly" carries two testable conditions, not
// one: who appoints, and under what regulations. An appointment made outside
// General-Assembly-established regulations does not satisfy the paragraph, so
// the rule reads both facts and the negative case is real. Paragraph 2 (the
// permanent assignment of staffs to ECOSOC, the Trusteeship Council and other
// organs) is organizational allocation with no addressee-conduct condition —
// the same register as Article 7's organs clause. Paragraph 3's "paramount
// consideration… the necessity of securing the highest standards of
// efficiency, competence, and integrity" and its "Due regard shall be paid to
// the importance of recruiting the staff on as wide a geographical basis as
// possible" were both checked against DECISION-0194's own test and are NOT
// channels: neither sentence names any organ or officer as the holder of the
// balance between the two considerations (contrast Article 99's "in his
// opinion", Article 31's "the Council considers", Article 14's "it deems"),
// so making either an `external judgment relation` would invent a judge the
// text does not appoint, and making them bare case facts would leave an
// evaluative fact with no producer. Both are left as unformalized residue
// inside an EXECUTABLE article — the same treatment as Article 32's second
// sentence, Article 36's paragraph 2 and Article 97's third sentence.

language "law.core" version "0.2";
package un.charter version "0.1.0";
namespace "urn:un:corpus:clir:charter";

// The package's first officer entity — see the header comment for why the
// Article 8/9 entity-gap boundary does not extend to Chapter XV.
entity SecretariatOfficial { label en official "The Secretary-General or a member of the staff of the Secretariat (Ch. XV)"; }

// --- Article 97: the Secretariat, and the appointment of the Secretary-General

pub relation security_council_recommends_secretary_general(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind empirical {
    label en official "the Security Council recommends the appointment of the person as Secretary-General (Art. 97) — itself an Article 27, para. 3 decision: a single permanent member's negative vote blocks the recommendation";
}

pub relation general_assembly_appoints_secretary_general(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind empirical {
    label en official "the General Assembly appoints the person as Secretary-General (Art. 97)";
}

pub relation secretary_general(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind institutional {
    label en unofficial "the person is the Secretary-General of the Organization (Art. 97)";
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART97)
rule SecretaryGeneralAppointedByGeneralAssemblyOnCouncilRecommendation(p: SecretariatOfficial) strict {
    label en official "Article 97, second sentence. The Secretary-General shall be appointed by the General Assembly upon the recommendation of the Security Council.";
    when security_council_recommends_secretary_general(p)
        and general_assembly_appoints_secretary_general(p);
    then secretary_general(p);
}

// A SEPARATE positive predicate for the negative side, not a negation of
// `secretary_general`: the same canon as Article 4's
// `admission_blocked_without_recommendation` and Article 5/6's `*_denied`.
pub relation security_council_recommendation_for_secretary_general_blocked(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind empirical {
    label en official "the Security Council's recommendation of the person as Secretary-General is blocked by a permanent member (Art. 97 — veto via Art. 27, para. 3)";
}

pub relation secretary_general_appointment_blocked(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind institutional {
    label en unofficial "the appointment is impossible without a Security Council recommendation, however willing the General Assembly (Art. 97, negative side)";
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART97)
rule SecretaryGeneralAppointmentBlockedWithoutRecommendation(p: SecretariatOfficial) strict {
    label en official "Article 97, second sentence, negative side: without a Security Council recommendation there is no appointment for the General Assembly to make.";
    when security_council_recommendation_for_secretary_general_blocked(p);
    then secretary_general_appointment_blocked(p);
}

// Article 97, first sentence — the Secretariat's population, DERIVED from its
// two branches (see the header comment), read by Article 100's duty below.

pub relation member_of_secretariat(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind institutional {
    label en unofficial "the person is of the Secretariat: the Secretary-General or a member of the staff (Art. 97, first sentence)";
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART97)
rule SecretariatComprisesTheSecretaryGeneral(p: SecretariatOfficial) strict {
    label en official "Article 97, first sentence, first branch. The Secretariat shall comprise a Secretary-General…";
    when secretary_general(p);
    then member_of_secretariat(p);
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART97)
rule SecretariatComprisesTheStaff(p: SecretariatOfficial) strict {
    label en official "Article 97, first sentence, second branch. …and such staff as the Organization may require.";
    when staff_member(p);
    then member_of_secretariat(p);
}

// --- Article 98: the annual report to the General Assembly ------------------

pub relation annual_report_on_work_of_organization_made(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind empirical {
    label en official "the Secretary-General has made his annual report to the General Assembly on the work of the Organization (Art. 98, second sentence)";
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART98)
rule DutyToMakeAnnualReportOnWorkOfOrganization(p: SecretariatOfficial) strict {
    label en official "Article 98, second sentence. The Secretary-General shall make an annual report to the General Assembly on the work of the Organization.";
    when secretary_general(p);
    then duty MakeAnnualReportOnWorkOfOrganization {
        bearer p;
        achieve annual_report_on_work_of_organization_made(p) during [@0001-01-01, infinity);
    };
}

// --- Article 99: bringing a matter to the Security Council's attention -----

pub external judgment relation matter_may_threaten_peace_in_opinion_of_secretary_general(p: SecretariatOfficial, m: SecurityCouncilMatter) {
    authority SecretaryGeneral;
    label en official "the matter may, in the opinion of the Secretary-General, threaten the maintenance of international peace and security (Art. 99)";
}

pub relation entitled_to_bring_matter_to_security_council_attention(p: SecretariatOfficial, m: SecurityCouncilMatter) kind institutional {
    label en unofficial "the Secretary-General may bring the matter to the attention of the Security Council (Art. 99)";
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART99)
rule SecretaryGeneralMayBringMatterToSecurityCouncilAttention(p: SecretariatOfficial, m: SecurityCouncilMatter) strict {
    label en official "Article 99. The Secretary-General may bring to the attention of the Security Council any matter which in his opinion may threaten the maintenance of international peace and security.";
    when secretary_general(p)
        and matter_may_threaten_peace_in_opinion_of_secretary_general(p, m);
    then entitled_to_bring_matter_to_security_council_attention(p, m);
}

// --- Article 100: independence, in both directions -------------------------

pub relation independence_of_office_maintained(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind empirical {
    label en official "the officer has sought and received no instructions from any government or other authority external to the Organization, and has refrained from any action which might reflect on his position as an international official (Art. 100, para. 1)";
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART100)
rule DutyOfIndependenceOfTheSecretariat(p: SecretariatOfficial) strict {
    label en official "Article 100, paragraph 1. In the performance of their duties the Secretary-General and the staff shall not seek or receive instructions from any government or from any other authority external to the Organization. They shall refrain from any action which might reflect on their position as international officials responsible only to the Organization.";
    when member_of_secretariat(p);
    then duty MaintainIndependenceOfOffice {
        bearer p;
        achieve independence_of_office_maintained(p) during [@0001-01-01, infinity);
    };
}

pub relation respects_international_character_of_secretariat(s: State, p: SecretariatOfficial) kind empirical {
    label en official "the Member has respected the exclusively international character of the officer's responsibilities and has not sought to influence him in their discharge (Art. 100, para. 2)";
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART100)
rule DutyToRespectInternationalCharacterOfTheSecretariat(s: State, p: SecretariatOfficial) strict {
    label en official "Article 100, paragraph 2. Each Member of the United Nations undertakes to respect the exclusively international character of the responsibilities of the Secretary-General and the staff and not to seek to influence them in the discharge of their responsibilities.";
    when member_state(s)
        and member_of_secretariat(p);
    then duty RespectInternationalCharacterOfTheSecretariat {
        bearer s;
        achieve respects_international_character_of_secretariat(s, p) during [@0001-01-01, infinity);
    };
}

// --- Article 101: appointment of the staff --------------------------------

pub relation appointed_to_staff_by_secretary_general(p: SecretariatOfficial, sg: SecretariatOfficial) kind empirical {
    label en official "the person has been appointed to the staff by the Secretary-General (Art. 101, para. 1)";
}

pub relation appointment_under_general_assembly_staff_regulations(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind empirical {
    label en official "the appointment is made under the staff regulations established by the General Assembly (Art. 101, para. 1)";
}

pub relation staff_member(p: SecretariatOfficial) kind institutional {
    label en unofficial "the person is a member of the staff of the Secretariat (Art. 101, para. 1)";
}

@source(UNC_EN_ART101)
rule StaffAppointedBySecretaryGeneralUnderAssemblyRegulations(p: SecretariatOfficial, sg: SecretariatOfficial) strict {
    label en official "Article 101, paragraph 1. The staff shall be appointed by the Secretary-General under regulations established by the General Assembly.";
    when secretary_general(sg)
        and appointed_to_staff_by_secretary_general(p, sg)
        and appointment_under_general_assembly_staff_regulations(p);
    then staff_member(p);
}
